###############################
# Git Line Endings            #
###############################

# LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Linux and macOS are where this is written and where it is deployed; Windows is
# an exception endured to keep the code portable. So the line ending is LF
# everywhere, and a Windows checkout gets LF too rather than each machine's
# `core.autocrlf` deciding, a per-machine setting nothing here records.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than bare `text=auto` is the difference between normalising
# what is *stored* and normalising what is *on disk*. The gap between the two is
# where the damage lives: in one session it produced `sed -i` silently rewriting
# a file whose CRLF was load-bearing, a shell script committed with a `bash\r`
# shebang that would have failed on a runner with "bad interpreter", and a
# warning on nearly every `git add`.
#
# This marks nothing as modified. Git compares normalised content, so a CRLF
# working tree still matches an LF index; files convert as git next touches
# them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The exceptions that genuinely need CRLF on disk, because their interpreter is
# a Windows one and they are Windows artefacts by definition.
*.[cC][mM][dD] text eol=crlf
*.[bB][aA][tT] text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Redundant under the rule above, and kept because it is the one that bit.
*.sh text eol=lf

###############################
# Binaries                    #
###############################

# Declared binary so git neither diffs them nor touches their bytes. Without a
# rule git guesses per file from its contents, and a guess that happens to be
# right is not a rule.
#
# Deliberately NOT `filter=lfs`. Only `fixconnector` actually uses LFS in this
# workspace: 19 objects, which is why it keeps the filters. Everywhere else the
# filters were declared and never fired, which is worse than not having them: the
# first binary somebody adds is converted silently, and from then on the
# repository needs `git-lfs` to yield its own contents. `scxml` is already
# half-way into that state, carrying a PNG as an ordinary object under a
# declaration that says it should be LFS.
#
# That matters most where these repositories are going. An escrow recipient
# without `git-lfs` gets pointer files: the right names, the right count, and
# none of the bytes.
#
# Adopting LFS is a decision with a migration and a quota attached. It should be
# taken deliberately, for a repository that has a reason, and not inherited by
# copying this file.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.psd binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.7z binary
*.br binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.duckdb binary
*.db binary
